     "t": "code",
     "lang": "python",
     "code": "\"\"\"\nDUCK TYPING\n=============\n\n\"If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck.\"\n\nPython doesn't check types — it checks behavior. If an object has\nthe required methods/attributes, it can be used regardless of its type.\n\nThis is fundamental to Python's design and enables:\n  - Polymorphism without inheritance\n  - Flexible APIs\n  - Protocol-based programming\n\"\"\"\n\n\nclass FileWriter:\n    def write(self, data):\n        return f\"File: wrote '{data}'\"\n\n\nclass NetworkWriter:\n    def write(self, data):\n        return f\"Network: sent '{data}'\"\n\n\nclass StringWriter:\n    def write(self, data):\n        return f\"String: stored '{data}'\"\n\n\ndef save_data(writer, data):\n    \"\"\"Works with ANY object that has a write() method.\"\"\"\n    return writer.write(data)\n\n\ndef demo_duck_typing():\n    writers = [FileWriter(), NetworkWriter(), StringWriter()]\n    for writer in writers:\n        print(f\"  {save_data(writer, 'hello')}\")\n\n    # Even a lambda works if it has the right interface\n    class LambdaWriter:\n        write = lambda self, data: f\"Lambda: {data}\"\n\n    print(f\"  {save_data(LambdaWriter(), 'test')}\")\n\n\nprint(\"Q167: Duck Typing\")\ndemo_duck_typing()\nprint()",
